<h2>Rows and columns</h2>

<p>
  A slide is one column by default: <b>title, text, code</b>, top left. Two tags change that. A <c><row></c> puts its children <b>side by side</b>
  and divides the width between them; a <c><col></c> <b>stacks</b> its children. Nest them for any layout, and the parts always add up to
  the whole slide.
</p>

<code lang=text>
  <h2>Title</h2>                 <- on top

  <row>                          <- below it, side by side:
    <p>The text on the left</p>
    <code lang=ts>...\</code>     <- the code on the right
  </row>

  <row>
    <col>                        <- a col: several things in one part
      <p>a</p>
      <img src=x.png />
    </col>
    <p>b</p>
    <p size=2>c</p>              <- twice as wide
  </row>
</code>

<p>
  Every direct child of a row is a part; use a <c><col></c> to put several things into one. <c>size=2</c> is a weight, <c>size=auto</c>
  the size of the content, <c>size=40%</c> a length. Add <c>halign</c> or <c>valign</c> to align inside a part.
</p>

<notes>
  Slide settings: layout: cover for a title slide. margin: and gap: in the slide block set the space around and between the parts.
</notes>
